{"name":"In the Mood","hex":"#2178a4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"in the mood","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Prepositional phrase","definitions":["Disposed (to do something or for something; often romantic or sexual, without further context)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/in_the_mood"}],"entries":[{"title":"In the Mood","blocks":[{"text":"\"In the Mood\" is a popular big band-era jazz standard recorded by American bandleader Glenn Miller. \"In the Mood\" is based on the composition \"Tar Paper Stomp\" by Wingy Manone. The first recording under the name \"In the Mood\" was released by Edgar Hayes and his Orchestra in 1938."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/2/2b/Inmood2-1-.JPG/330px-Inmood2-1-.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/In_the_Mood","wikidata":"Q1413725"},{"title":"In the Mood (film)","blocks":[{"text":"In the Mood is a 1987 American comedy film written and directed by Phil Alden Robinson. The film is based on the true story of Sonny Wisecarver. Set in the 1940s, the feature stars Patrick Dempsey, Beverly D'Angelo, Michael Constantine, Betty Jinnette, Kathleen Freeman, and Peter Hobbs. The film opened on September 16, 1987 in New York City and on September 18, 1987 in Los Angeles, California and Toronto, Canada. When the movie expanded to 361 screens on October 16, 1987, it took in $315,000 in its first three days of wider release."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/0a/InTheMood%28film%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/In_the_Mood_(film)","wikidata":"Q6012566"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c9/In_the_Mood_Ernie_Fields.jpg/250px-In_the_Mood_Ernie_Fields.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/c9/In_the_Mood_Ernie_Fields.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c9/In_the_Mood_Ernie_Fields.jpg/250px-In_the_Mood_Ernie_Fields.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIn_the_Mood_Ernie_Fields.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/da/In_the_mood_sheet_music_glenn_miller.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/da/In_the_mood_sheet_music_glenn_miller.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"The 1939 sheet music cover, \"Introduced by Glenn Miller\", Shapiro, Bernstein, and Co., New York","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIn_the_mood_sheet_music_glenn_miller.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/V_Disc_123B_In_the_Mood_Glenn_Miller.jpg/500px-V_Disc_123B_In_the_Mood_Glenn_Miller.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/V_Disc_123B_In_the_Mood_Glenn_Miller.jpg/500px-V_Disc_123B_In_the_Mood_Glenn_Miller.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"V-Disc release","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AV_Disc_123B_In_the_Mood_Glenn_Miller.jpg"}],"sounds":{}}}